Q) Difference between @Qualifier and @Primary in Spring Framework
=> "@Primary and @Qualifier are used when multiple Spring beans of the same type exist. @Primary marks one bean as the default choice,
    whereas @Qualifier explicitly tells Spring which specific bean should be injected."

Q) How security handling is done in project. Which security framework is used (JWT, OAuth2.0) ?  Working on JWT or OAuth2.0.
=>  In my project, we use Spring Security with JWT-based authentication. When the user authenticates successfully,
    the server generates a signed JWT containing relevant claims such as the user identity and authorities.
    The client sends the JWT with subsequent requests using the Authorization: Bearer <token> header.
    Spring Security intercepts the request through its security filter chain. The JWT is validated, including its signature
    and relevant claims such as expiration. If valid, Spring Security establishes the authenticated principal and authorization rules
    determine whether the user has permission to access the endpoint.

Q) How to secure sensitive data in Spring Boot application (Application.properties). How to encrypt and decrypt sensitive data.
=>  I avoid storing sensitive credentials such as database passwords, API keys, and secret keys directly in application.properties
    or application.yml. Instead, I externalize them using environment variables, Docker/Kubernetes secrets,
    or a dedicated secrets manager such as AWS Secrets Manager or Azure Key Vault.
    If encryption at rest is required, the secret can be stored encrypted and decrypted by a trusted component at runtime.
    The encryption key itself should never be stored alongside the encrypted value; it should be managed separately using a
    secure key-management or secrets system.
    Application.properties file :
        spring.datasource.username=${DB_USERNAME}
        spring.datasource.password=${DB_PASSWORD}
    Then the deployment environment provides:
        DB_USERNAME=app_user
        DB_PASSWORD=********

    Q) Can we Base64 encode the password?"
        No. Base64 is encoding, not encryption. It provides no confidentiality.

Q) Explain stages in CI/CD (Build, docker push, deploy etc)
    Our CI/CD pipeline automatically takes code from source control through build, testing, packaging, containerization,
    image publishing, deployment, and verification.

    Checkout: The pipeline starts by checking out the latest code from the version control system (e.g., Git).
    Build: The code is compiled, and dependencies are resolved. This may involve running unit tests to ensure code quality.
    Unit Test: Automated tests are executed to validate the functionality of the application.
    Package: The application is packaged into a deployable format, such as a JAR.
    Containerization: The packaged application is built into a Docker image, which includes the application and its runtime environment.
    Docker Push: The Docker image is pushed to a container registry (e.g., Docker Hub, AWS ECR, or Azure Container Registry) for storage and distribution.
    Deploy: The Docker image is deployed to the target environment (e.g., staging or production) using orchestration tools like Kubernetes or Docker Compose.
    Verify: Post-deployment, automated tests or health checks are run to ensure the application is functioning correctly in the target environment.

