Q) Explain Solid Principles with examples.
    SOLID helps us create code that is maintainable, extensible, testable, and loosely coupled.
    In Spring applications, Dependency Inversion is especially visible through dependency injection, where services depend on
    interfaces rather than concrete implementations.
    S — Single Responsibility Principle
        A class should have only one reason to change. For example, a UserService class should only handle user-related operations,
        not email notifications or logging.
        Ex : OrderService
             EmailService
             InvoiceService
    O — Open/Closed Principle
        Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification.
        For example, if you want to add a new payment method, you can create a new class that implements a PaymentMethod interface
        instead of modifying existing payment classes.
    L — Liskov Substitution Principle
        Subtypes must be substitutable for their base types. The interface should represent behavior that all implementations can actually support.
        Ex :
            class Bird {
                void fly() {}
            }
            class Penguin extends Bird {
                // Penguin cannot fly and  abstraction is wrong here. So, we should not have fly() method in Bird class.
            }
    I — Interface Segregation Principle
        Clients should not be forced to depend on interfaces they do not use. For example, instead of having a single large interface for all types of vehicles, create separate interfaces for Car, Bike, and Truck.
    D — Dependency Inversion Principle
        High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions. For example, instead of a CustomerService class directly depending on a MySQLDatabase class, it should depend on a Database interface.

Q) Explain difference between application.properties and application.yml in Spring Boot. What if both are present in the project.
=>  "Both are supported configuration formats in Spring Boot. application.properties uses key-value properties, while application.yml
    or application.yaml represents configuration in a hierarchical YAML structure.
    Functionally, they can represent the same configuration; the choice is mainly readability and project preference."
    Properties file example:
        server.port=8080
        spring.datasource.url=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/app
        spring.datasource.username=admin
    YAML file example:
        server:
          port: 8080
        spring:
          datasource:
            url: jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/app
            username: admin

Q) Can we use both application.properties and application.yml in the same Spring Boot project? If yes, which one will take precedence?
=>  Spring Boot can have both application.properties and application.yml/application.yaml. Configuration from both can be loaded.
    If the same property is defined in both, the property with higher precedence wins; when they are in the same application
    configuration location, application.properties takes precedence over YAML. So generally, we should avoid defining the same
    configuration in both files to prevent confusion.
    application.properties overrides application.yml/application.yaml when both define the same property at the same configuration location.

Q) Have 1 HashMap with null key. Put key null and value as "value1". Then put key as null and value as "value2".
    What will be the final value of the null key in the HashMap?
=>  HashMap allows one null key. When we put another value using the same null key, it replaces the existing value.
    Therefore, the final value is value2.

Q) Time complexity of HashMap operations (get, put, remove) in Java.
=>  "For a HashMap, get, put, and remove are O(1) on average. In the worst case, collisions can degrade performance.
    In modern Java implementations, heavily collided buckets can be treeified, giving O(log n) lookup within that bucket under
    the relevant conditions."

Q) What is zookeeper in Kafka? How it is used in Kafka? What is the role of zookeeper in Kafka?
=> Historically, Kafka used ZooKeeper for cluster metadata and coordination. ZooKeeper managed information such as broker membership,
    controller election, and cluster metadata. However, modern Kafka supports KRaft mode, where Kafka manages its metadata internally
    without requiring ZooKeeper. So for a new Kafka deployment, I would first check whether the cluster uses ZooKeeper or KRaft.

