Q) Spring Boot Application takes a long time to start because of heavy bean initialization.
=>  First, I would identify which beans are causing the startup delay rather than immediately making everything lazy.
    I would inspect startup metrics/logs and identify expensive initialization such as database connections, external API calls,
    large cache loading, or expensive object creation. Then I would move non-essential work out of startup,
    initialize it asynchronously or on demand, and optimize the expensive initialization.
    What I'd investigate :
        1. Startup logs
        2. Bean initialization time
        3. DB connection initialization
        4. External API calls
        5. Large cache loading
        6. Expensive @PostConstruct logic
        7. Entity scanning / JPA initialization
        8. Auto-configurations

    Approach to optimize :
        1. Use @Lazy for non-essential beans
        2. Use @ConditionalOnProperty or @ConditionalOnClass to conditionally load beans
        3. Use @Profile to load beans only in specific environments
        4. Use asynchronous initialization for heavy beans
        5. Optimize expensive initialization logic
        6. Use caching for expensive computations
        7. Reduce the number of auto-configurations by excluding unnecessary ones

